notes2: Founder Q — "iPhone mic + Bluetooth headphones for better live monitoring works too, right?" Yes: selecting the iPhone mic (low-latency, full-quality) while BT headphones carry the OUTPUT (A2DP) is the better of the BT options — but A2DP output still adds ~150–250 ms, so self-monitoring stays delayed (the latency is on the OUTPUT side). The Input sheet now reads the live output route (AudioInputManager.outputRouteName / outputIsHighLatency) and, when monitoring through a Bluetooth/AirPlay/CarPlay output, shows an honest amber note explaining the delay and pointing to wired/USB for delay-free monitoring. The per-input latency badge alone was misleading for this combo.
notes: Founder request — "Baue das System für Akku, CPU, GPU für Ressourcenschonungen ein. Adaptive Qualität und Formate" + "Visuals brauchen viel Aufmerksamkeit damit alles ruckelfrei funktioniert". New AdaptiveQuality (pure, Linux-unit-tested) maps device pressure — thermal state, Low Power Mode, battery level/charging, and the measured render FPS — to one app-wide QualitySettings (visual targetFPS, detail scale, reduce-motion, spectral-donut allowance, bio/OSC rates). Worst-active-constraint wins; reaches High only when charging AND cool; a sustained FPS shortfall (<75% of target) demotes one tier so the GPU is never oversubscribed. ResourceGovernor (@MainActor @Observable) reads ProcessInfo/UIDevice + thermal/power/battery notifications (Darwin-gated; Linux/sim treat as on-power/nominal) and publishes the live tier. MetalBioView now honours it: preferredFramesPerSecond, ring-density × detailScale, and reduce-motion all follow the governor, and each drawn frame feeds its timestamp back (main-thread MainActor.assumeIsolated) so a struggling device steps down BEFORE the OS throttles. Concurrency-reviewed (0 issues). Visuals stay ruckelfrei; battery/heat are conserved automatically with zero user action. Manual tier-pin UI + bio/OSC-rate consumers are the documented next adopters. iOS verifies on device; compile gate validates.
